[
    {
        "content": "<p>It is difficult to understand the exact semantics and behavior of def declarations in Mathlib. From the perspective of lambda calculus, a definition should represent either a function or a symbolic name associated with a term. Its mathematical meaning should remain consistent regardless of where it is declared or used.</p>\n<p>However, in Lean, def declarations appear to exhibit behavior that he depends on more than their explicit definitions. When I copy a def declaration from Mathlib into my own Lean file and use it in a similar context, I sometimes obtain different results.</p>",
        "id": 632939801,
        "sender_full_name": "Linfeng Song",
        "timestamp": 1792400245
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Could you provide an <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a> for what you mean?</p>",
        "id": 632941270,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1792400569
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This topic was moved here from <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"287929\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Question.3A.20Lean.20def.20behavior\">#mathlib4 &gt; Question: Lean def behavior</a> by <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"634338\">Michael Rothgang</span>.</p>",
        "id": 632947648,
        "sender_full_name": "Notification Bot",
        "timestamp": 1792402029
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here is simple example, the real case when using mathlib4 might be much difficult to determine by import modules </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">double</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">double</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">double</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">double</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">—</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fail</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">as</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">expected</span>\n\n\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">double_eq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">double</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">double</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">—</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">success</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">now</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">That</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">is</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">confused</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 632950165,
        "sender_full_name": "Linfeng Song",
        "timestamp": 1792402703
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib/wiki/Code-in-backticks\">#backticks</a> for formatting.<br>\nSo to clarify, your question is how do <code>@[simp]</code> tags work?</p>",
        "id": 632951677,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1792403119
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Question: original def statement has different behavior in different environment. How to understand it or where is document?</p>",
        "id": 632954258,
        "sender_full_name": "Linfeng Song",
        "timestamp": 1792403735
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No, the difference between your two examples is <code>@[simp]</code>, entirely unrelated to <code>def</code></p>",
        "id": 632957951,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1792404627
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"933054\">@Snir Broshi</span> I would more broadly interpret this as a question about the difference between \"pure\" terms in a lambda calculus and the implicit state in a proof assistant that affects instances, tactics, etc. </p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"972470\">@Linfeng Song</span> As Snir said, it is not accurate to say that the definition has different behavior. What you are seeing is that the <code>simp</code> tactic, which you should think of as a metaprogram for manipulating terms rather than a term itself, does have behavior depending on what theorems or definitions have been given the <code>simp</code> attribute. In this case you could use the <code>simp?</code> tactic with your second example, and this will show you what was used to construct this proof. Your observation that such behaviors can depend on imports is correct.</p>",
        "id": 632958781,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1792404841
    }
]